   NAME
	LhX - Lean AmigaDOS LHA archiver (lh.library client).

   FORMAT
	LhX LIST|EXTRACT|EXTRACTFLAT|ADD|TEST|PRINT ARCHIVE
	     [{<file|pattern>}] [TO <dir>] [QUIET] [FORCE] [NOPATHS]
	     [PASSWORD <pass>] [HELP]

   TEMPLATE
	L=LIST/S,X=EXTRACT/S,EXTRACTFLAT/S,A=ADD/S,T=TEST/S,PRINT/S,
	HELP/S,ARCHIVE,FILES/M,TO/K,QUIET/S,FORCE/S,NP=NOPATH=NOPATHS/S,
	PASSWORD/K

   PATH
	C:LhX

   FUNCTION
	LhX is the LhASsA native AmigaDOS front end for archives. It
	uses ReadArgs, AmigaDOS wildcards, and lh.library rather than
	the classic LhA letter-option syntax. Exactly one operation
	switch must be given together with ARCHIVE.

	LIST prints archive members in a LIST-like layout (name, size,
	protection, date, time, and filenote when present). EXTRACT
	restores files with stored paths; EXTRACTFLAT (or EXTRACT with
	NOPATHS) restores base names only. ADD creates or updates an
	archive from FILES patterns, storing each file under the path
	given on the command line (on-disk capitalisation; volume ':'
	becomes '/') unless NOPATHS is set. Same-named members are
	replaced. TEST checks member CRC integrity. PRINT writes
	selected member data to the current output.

	FILES arguments may use AmigaDOS wildcards such as #? and *.
	For LIST, EXTRACT, and TEST, ARCHIVE may also be a filesystem
	wildcard; each matching archive is processed in turn. ADD and
	PRINT require a single concrete ARCHIVE path. Ctrl-C aborts
	cleanly between files. When launched from Workbench as a
	project default tool for a .lha file, LhX remaps the archive
	onto the LHA: volume (Lh-handler) and opens it as a drawer when
	the handler is mounted; otherwise it extracts to T:LhX/ with a
	progress requester.

	Unlike classic LhA a, LhX ADD keeps full paths by default and
	replaces duplicates. Use LhA when you need 2.15 letter options,
	home directories, or -r recursion.

   OPTIONS
	LIST (L)
	    List the contents of ARCHIVE. With QUIET, only names are
	    printed. ARCHIVE may be a wildcard matching several
	    archives.

	EXTRACT (X)
	    Extract members. Paths from the archive are recreated unless
	    NOPATHS or EXTRACTFLAT is used. TO sets the destination
	    directory. ARCHIVE may be a wildcard matching several
	    archives.

	EXTRACTFLAT
	    Extract members using base names only (no directory path).

	ADD (A)
	    Add FILES from the filesystem into ARCHIVE. Each member is
	    stored with the full path as given (volume ':' becomes '/'),
	    using on-disk capitalisation for each component, unless
	    NOPATHS is set. A member that already exists under the same
	    name (case-insensitive) is replaced. ARCHIVE must be a
	    single path (no wildcards).

	TEST (T)
	    Test CRC integrity of members in ARCHIVE. ARCHIVE may be a
	    wildcard matching several archives.

	PRINT
	    Print selected member contents to the current output.
	    ARCHIVE must be a single path.

	ARCHIVE
	    Path of the .lha / .lzh archive (required). For LIST,
	    EXTRACT, and TEST this may be an AmigaDOS pattern that
	    expands to one or more archive files. For ADD and PRINT it
	    must name a single archive.

	FILES
	    Zero or more file names or patterns. For LIST, EXTRACT,
	    TEST, and PRINT these select archive members. For ADD they
	    are filesystem paths/patterns to store. If omitted, all
	    members are selected for list/extract/test/print. ADD with
	    no FILES is an error (unlike classic LhA a, which means
	    "all files in the current directory").

	TO <dir>
	    Destination directory for EXTRACT / EXTRACTFLAT.

	QUIET
	    Reduce console chatter (list shows names only).

	FORCE
	    Overwrite existing files on extract when allowed by
	    protection bits.

	NOPATHS (NOPATH, NP)
	    On extract: ignore stored paths (same effect as EXTRACTFLAT
	    for path handling). On ADD: store basename only instead of
	    the full command-line path, and replace any existing member
	    with that same basename (including older path-form names).

	PASSWORD <pass>
	    Password for protected archives (passed to lh.library;
	    support is limited).

	HELP
	    Print this help text from HELP:<locale>/LhASsA/LhX.help
	    (falls back to HELP:english/LhASsA/LhX.help), otherwise the
	    short usage line, then exit successfully.

	You must specify exactly one of LIST, EXTRACT, EXTRACTFLAT,
	ADD, TEST, or PRINT.

   EXAMPLES
	    1> LhX LIST Archive.lha

	Lists every member of Archive.lha.

	    1> LhX LIST RAM:#?.lha

	Lists every archive in RAM: whose name matches #?.lha.

	    1> LhX EXTRACT Archive.lha TO=RAM:Out/

	Extracts all members into RAM:Out/, recreating paths.

	    1> LhX EXTRACTFLAT Archive.lha #?.doc TO=RAM:

	Extracts only .doc members, flattened into RAM:.

	    1> LhX ADD Backup.lha SYS/C/LhX

	Adds SYS/C/LhX into Backup.lha as member SYS/C/LhX (replacing
	any existing member of that name).

	    1> LhX ADD Backup.lha Work:Sources/#? QUIET

	Adds matching source files into Backup.lha, keeping each
	matched path (with ':' normalised to '/').

	    1> LhX ADD Backup.lha SYS/C/LhX NOPATHS

	Adds the same file as member LhX (basename only).

	    1> LhX TEST Archive.lha

	Verifies CRC of every member.

	    1> LhX PRINT Archive.lha ReadMe

	Writes the ReadMe member to the current output.

   SEE ALSO
	LhA, Load, Handle
